// Tutorial 98: Users — who's on this computer
//
// Run it:      cabby 098-users.cby
// Or compile:  cabby -c 098-users.cby && ./098-users
// Library:     use users
// Topics:      users.name, full_name, home, shell, id, is_root, groups,
//              in_group; users.exists, full_name_of, home_of, groups_of;
//              users.all, all_groups (and, in the chapter, making users and
//              groups, which needs the administrator)
//
// Everyone who logs in to a computer is a user, with a login name, a home
// folder, and groups: what they're allowed to do (`sudo` to be the
// administrator, `audio` to play sound on some Linuxes). This program says
// who's running it, and who else is here. Making and removing users needs
// the administrator, so that's in the chapter.

use users

int main {
	println "You're", users.name, "(" + users.full_name + "), number", users.id
	println "Your home is", users.home, "and your shell", users.shell
	println "Your groups:", users.groups
	if users.is_root {
		println "You're the administrator (root): careful!"
	} else if users.in_group("sudo") || users.in_group("wheel") || users.in_group("admin") {
		println "You can be the administrator, with sudo"
	}
	for Name in users.all {
		if Name != users.name {
			println "Also here:", Name, "(" + users.full_name_of(Name) + "), at", users.home_of(Name)
		}
	}
	println "There's a root:", users.exists("root"), "and a nobody-at-all:", users.exists("nobody-at-all")
	println users.all_groups.length, "groups in all"
}

// Try this:
//  - Print each user's groups (users.groups_of).
//  - Say which users are in the same groups as you.
//  - As the administrator (sudo cabby ...): make a user "guest", give them
//    a group of their own, and remove them again (see the chapter).

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- output
// output varies: what one computer said about its users (run as root; a few dozen of its build users left out)
//> You're root (root), number 0
//> Your home is /root and your shell /bin/bash
//> Your groups: [root]
//> You're the administrator (root): careful!
//> Also here: ubuntu (Ubuntu), at /home/ubuntu
//> There's a root: true and a nobody-at-all: false
//> 49 groups in all
